so i know you're probably getting  asked about this more than anything but  i can't help it because the one  in this is absolutely incredible is  there any one particular beat of that  whole sequence that you found to be the  most challenging  do you know what probably the hardest  the hardest bit of it was was the very  beginning  because you know you hear action you  know that you've got about 30 minutes  with no cuts and you don't want to drop  the ball because if you do you have to  start again you let everyone down  but once you're in it it's like it's  like being on a roller coaster it's just  it's just like because you're free  falling you know you don't know what's  going to happen  most the lines are improvised you know  that you kind of have to be in this  this bit and then go here and then go to  the bath and go to the bedroom  but so it's probably the beginning  because you're just sort of easing in in  from  you know normal reality to sort of the  the play of it i guess  um so yeah i'd say that one because and  then so  you can't really think about that  anymore you can't your nerves just go  because you have to be present  or you know you might something might go  wrong  what do you do to recharge in between  takes cause i think i heard somewhere  you did it  like six times and i mean i i have a  hard enough time  just watching the scene once um  um well yeah because at the end it's  really it's really odd because  in a way you're i guess you're  unconscious like your psyche doesn't  know the difference between real and not  real so  i i took me quite a long time it took me  maybe 10 minutes to sort of come out of  it  and then you know everyone will be  resetting stuff and it's a bit it's a  bit like theater when you do an  afternoon show and then you have an  evening show and you have to kind of  work out how to reset so i'm so lucky  that i've done a lot of plays because  that really helped me but the one thing  that helped the most is we just i  remember we just  had this big speaker and we just blasted  music out you know it was always really  upbeat  like just to change the energy you know  just to make us feel like  that hadn't happened and we're going  back to the beginning and so we and we  danced around the kitchen all of us  and it was uh it just kind of like you  know you kind of like shook the energy  out so  that was it i'm glad to hear you had a  little bit of levity in between  we did yeah what about an unsung hero  of that sequence was there someone who i  don't know had to like  hide under the bed the entire time or  someone doing lighting and sound magic  to keep  equipment out of the shot just someone  that you know a name we don't know that  you really think deserves a shout out  right now  yes the prosthetics team who hid  in the cupboard of the bedroom and they  would have to  like when the i think the camera leaves  the bedroom  and then as the camera was outside the  bedroom while  sean was on the phone the the prosthetic  team would run out and they had to put  you know the baby's head coming out  basically so they had to quickly put  that there so that when the camera came  back they would find it  and the other unsung hero is the actual  baby who was waiting around the corner  with his  parents in the other room and um  [Music]  and he'd be brought in and would be  suddenly there in the scene and so he  was  he was being super quiet and uh we  couldn't have done it without them  so um here is for sure  i still can't get over the whole thing  what do you find  more challenging doing a complicated one  or with all that movement like that  or doing a big emotional beat that's  completely static like the material in  the courtroom  yeah definitely definitely the material  in the courtroom  because i realized having done the birth  i realized that it was  like as you said it was so physical and  it's so uh that your body just has to do  it in a way  your mind doesn't get in the way and i  think one of the biggest challenges of  being an actor is  in the scenes anyway is um  is is trying not to let your mind come  in and  tell you or not doing well or  something's just gone wrong or  you know get in the way or like be  self-conscious and nervous which is  which is so hard to combat most the time  um and so the courtroom is way  way harder way harder to  wrap up here i did want to ask you about  filming  mission impossible just because  everyone's trying to adapt right now and  having had the experience filming during  the pandemic what would you say is  something done on that set that made you  feel like you could deliver your best  possible work  while staying safe that you'd like to  see on more sets now in the future  yeah i think i mean the protocols you  know you get pretty used to them quite  quickly  everyone's always two meters apart um  you know everyone's tested three or four  times a week  it's really rigorous and my sister's  also a member of the crew on the film  she's an ad  she she was on the first film backup  jurassic world in july i think and so i  got to see her do it and  and it was it was it was such a  wonderful moment because  you know the industry had to shut down  and  cinemas were closed and to see  the film set getting back up and running  and people being able to work was a  really  really beautiful thing

